Subject: secr for semi-closed boundaries
Message-ID: <cdIGTdSM9MY.ZfOrIpQYBQAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/secrgroup/c/cdIGTdSM9MY/m/ZfOrIpQYBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi. I am working with brown hyaena data in South Africa. The camera trap grids are in three different types of area - completely open (no fences; free movement), completely closed (impenetrable fences that are heavily maintained; no movement outside the reserve), and semi-closed (fenced areas that have penetrable holes that appear for awhile, holes are periodically closed up by rangers, new holes appear and these are closed, etc; restricted movement outside the reserve). In semi-closed areas, brown hyaenas can move in and out of the reserve (and predators do) but movement is not as easy as a completely open area. I suspect that if all other conditions were the same, density in a semi-closed area may be higher than a completely open area but not as high as a completely closed area. Do you think this is the case? I know how to run estimates for completely open areas and completely closed areas, but I was wondering if there is a technique in secr to accurately estimate density in a semi-closed area? If not, should I just run the semi-closed areas as if they were completely open (with an appropriate statespace that isn't restricted to the fenceline)? Thanks.

From u118cea91a1619106 at groups.google.com  Wed May 29 09:10:05 2019
From: u118cea91a1619106 at groups.google.com (Murray Efford)
Date: Wed, 29 May 2019 09:10:05 +0000
Subject: Re: secr for semi-closed boundaries
Message-ID: <cdIGTdSM9MY._aBHEgoaBQAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <cdIGTdSM9MY.ZfOrIpQYBQAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
References: <cdIGTdSM9MY.ZfOrIpQYBQAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/secrgroup/c/cdIGTdSM9MY/m/_aBHEgoaBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

There's no technique in secr that addresses this directly, at least as I understand the problem. I don't think we can answer the question about likely density differences without knowledge of the system - presumably there are gradients in prey density as well as fence effects and mysteries of hyaena behaviour.

As I understand it the holes are ephemeral and not documented, and that rules out directly modeling them using non-euclidean distance methods. Under a completely closed scenario all captures inside are of animals with activity centres inside, but under a partly closed scenario some may be intruders. How to measure the magnitude of this effect? I can't see that captures from only inside give any information on the fraction of animals that are intruders so you would have to infer that from other information (or guess it). One way to adjust the modelled proportion of intruders might be to impose a zero-density buffer around the reserve (i.e. drop an annulus of pixels from the habitat mask); the width of that buffer would control the frequency of incursion. But as there is no information on that frequency we're back to square one.

Sorry not to be more helpful.
Murray

